.TH CHMAP_DESTROY_WITH_DTOR 3 "2026-08-19" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chmap_destroy_with_dtor \- destroy a hash map, running a destructor on every value first, without allocating memory
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chashmap.h>

void chmap_destroy_with_dtor(chmap chm,
    void (*val_dtor)(const cmap_pair *val_pair, void *dtor_ctx),
    void *dtor_ctx);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Destroys the map exactly as chmap_destroy(3) does, with one difference:
when val_dtor is not NULL, this function calls it once for each live
entry, passing that entry's own value as a cmap_pair, directly before
it frees the entry.

This function never allocates memory of its own. It walks the map's own
internal storage directly, and the destructor call runs inside that same
walk, whereas a walk with chashmap_begin_iter(3) followed by a destroy
does allocate.

This function is meant for a map whose values are owned pointers to a
larger structure that needs its own recursive teardown, for example a
dictionary of dictionaries. Such a map can also be destroyed by walking
it with chashmap_begin_iter(3), running a value destructor for each
entry, and destroying the empty map at the end, but that approach has a
real gap under memory pressure: the small internal allocation of
chashmap_begin_iter(3) can itself fail, and then there is no route left
to reach and free the values that the map still owns. This function has
no such gap, because it does no allocation of its own.

The cmap_pair that val_dtor receives is the map's own accessor for that
entry, and its target is const, as in chmap_get_elem_ref(3). A
destructor reads val_pair->ptr and val_pair->size and then frees
whatever the value itself owns, usually a pointer copied out of those
bytes. An assignment to val_pair->ptr or val_pair->size is a compile
error.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
chm        Hash map to destroy
val_dtor   Destructor called once for each live value, before the
           function frees that entry, or NULL to destroy the map with
           no callback for each value. Its val_pair parameter is a
           const cmap_pair *
dtor_ctx   Opaque pointer forwarded unchanged to every val_dtor call
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
Safe to call with a NULL chm (a no-op)
Does not set the chm handle to NULL; use chmap_destroy(3) when the
handle must be cleared as well
val_dtor must not change chm itself, because the map is in the middle
of its teardown for the whole duration of this call
The accessor describes storage that the map is about to free, and the
map cannot own a pointer that it did not allocate itself. This is why
an assignment to either field of the accessor is a compile error, and
not only a rule to remember
Does no allocation of its own, so it cannot fail under any memory
condition that makes chashmap_begin_iter(3) itself fail
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chmap_destroy (3),
.BR chmap_get_elem_ref (3),
.BR chashmap_begin_iter (3)
